---
name: dev-task
version: 1.0.0
description: lead sprint assignment template for codex-orchestration development work.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - sprint_doc
  - description
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - pr_target
  - deliverables
  - acceptance_criteria
  - references
optional_variables:
  - lead
  - cc
  - dependency_relations
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  dependency_relations: "- See authoritative sprint doc."
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
{#- Temporary workaround for sc-compose #784: v1.7.0 rejects autoescape blocks. Revert these | safe filters once a release containing #788 is pinned. -#}
<atm-task id="{{ task_id | safe }}" sprint="{{ sprint | safe }}">
  <description>{{ description | safe }}</description>

  <worktree>{{ worktree_path | safe }}</worktree>
  <branch>{{ branch | safe }}</branch>
  <pr-target>{{ pr_target | safe }}</pr-target>
  <stack-discipline>`{{ branch | safe }}` is a layer cut from the current top of the phase stack; `{{ pr_target | safe }}` is the layer directly below it. Work only on this layer. Never edit, push to, rebase, or open a PR against any layer below, and never wait for a lower layer's QA or CI. If `{{ pr_target | safe }}` gains commits while you work, run `git fetch origin && git merge origin/{{ pr_target | safe }}` once at task start and once before the final push; the parent wins where copies differ. Once this task closes the layer is frozen: every later finding against it is fixed on a new layer cut from the top of the stack, never here.</stack-discipline>
  <authoritative-sprint-doc>{{ sprint_doc | safe }}</authoritative-sprint-doc>

  <dependency-relations>
{{ dependency_relations | safe }}
  </dependency-relations>

  <deliverables>
{{ deliverables | safe }}
  </deliverables>

  <acceptance-criteria>
{{ acceptance_criteria | safe }}
- sprint doc deliverables gate:
  - `{{ sprint_doc | safe }}` exists
  - sprint doc frontmatter sets `status: complete`
  - sprint doc frontmatter sets `branch:`
  - sprint doc frontmatter sets `worktree:`
- planning index gate:
  - `docs/project-plan.md` includes the sprint entry before closeout
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <references>
{{ references | safe }}
  </references>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a">Start the task immediately with `atm task start {{ task_id | safe }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`, then claim the matching bead with `bd update {{ task_id | safe }} --claim` and begin executing in the same work session. Make reasonable assumptions and do not ask clarifying questions unless a real blocker prevents execution.</step>
    <step id="c">Read `{{ sprint_doc | safe }}` before coding. Treat it as the authoritative source for scope, deliverables, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria. If this assignment summary and the sprint doc differ, the sprint doc wins and the mismatch must be reported.</step>
    <step id="d">Before coding, update the worktree from the sprint target branch and include prior fixes already merged to that target.</step>
    <step id="d1">Honor `<dependency-relations>`: must-follow merge-forward trigger is parent dev push, not QA; merge parent → child before every round. PR-completion trigger is parent PR merge first. Stop if a parallel-safe relation intersects.</step>
    <step id="e">As soon as the development changes are complete and the branch is pushable, commit and push immediately. Send branch name and commit hash right away to the assigner (the sender of this message) with a plain `atm send {{ lead | safe }} --stdin` — a progress report, not a close; never close the task on a push. This lets PR work and QA preparation begin in parallel. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc | safe }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | safe }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="f">After the push report, run the required validation and tests. Do not wait for PR creation before starting validation.</step>
    <step id="g">After validation, fix findings and re-run checks until clean. Then close the task with the completion report as the body: `atm task close {{ task_id | safe }} completed --stdin <<'EOF' … EOF`, followed by `bd close {{ task_id | safe }}` — the deliverable inventory and the exact gate output lines; for deletion or cleanup sprints, the before/after targeted symbol or code-path inventory (net LOC is secondary evidence). Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment stays queued and never starts until `{{ task_id | safe }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it. Use `atm task close {{ task_id | safe }} refused "<reason>"` only when the whole assignment cannot be done; leave its bead open and add `bd update {{ task_id | safe }} --notes "<reason>"`. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc | safe }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | safe }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="h">After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.</step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
{#- end of sc-compose #784 workaround -#}
